--- MESSAGE ---
The engine is DracoGenerator, and draco.Generator is free for the definition

Dev's naming for the generator domains, settled 2026-09-07/08:

    draco.Generator(T)                the transform from Draco to a target T — a definition-backed
                                      root type, parameterized by target
    draco.generator.Generator         the super-domain of every generator sub-domain (carrier
                                      stays under it: the input seam belongs to the super-domain)
    draco.genscala.GenScala           from Generator(ScalaTarget)  — the Draco -> Scala transform
    draco.gendrake.GenDrake           from Generator(DrakeTarget)  — the Draco -> drake emitter
    draco.scalatarget.ScalaTarget     a Target
    draco.draketarget.DrakeTarget     a Target — the emission side of the definition language
    draco.drake.Drake                 a Source — the definition language; parse belongs here

The forest scenario's Source_Target-under-source placement does not transfer: it degenerates when
the source is the root, would put two domain types in one package, and encodes a direction whose
inverse can never exist. So each transform is a peer package named for what it does, and the
Draco/draco prefixes are dropped where the package already says it.

The one inconsistency in that naming was a compile error: `draco.Generator` was taken, by the
hand-written engine in src/mods, which compiles into the same package. The typed domain had been put
at `draco.generator.Generator` precisely to coexist with it. The definition should own the name — the
engine is what GenScala's rules exist to replace — so the engine moves: `DracoGenerator`, Dev's
choice. GeneratorCLI and bin/draco-gen keep theirs; they are documented commands, not types.

Nothing else changes. The rename lands first because the first drake that authors
`draco.Generator(T)` triggers the collision; the six domains as data, with empty member lists, come
next, and then the structure the forest showed to be necessary — target-side types for the
morphisms to produce, a target-side receiver (a fact inserted by a rule gets no memory node without
one), an actor per transform to fire the rules, and parse as a member morphism of Drake so both legs
of the round trip live in the framework.

